r and must return incomplete. The `new_ids` check catches it on page 2. Good, and the `seen_pages` check is a second belt. Maybe just keep the `new_ids` check + seen key. Both are cheap. Keep both? \"Avoid over-engineering\". The `new_ids` check alone is sufficient for termination: each iteration must add ≥1 new PMID, so iterations ≤ count. Plus `max_pages` budget. I'll keep just the `new_ids` guard and the max_pages budget — simpler. Hmm, but \"重复游标\" naming... the diagnostic will say 重复分页游标. Fine.\n\nHmm, but what if idlist is empty while count > len(pmids)? → incomplete (\"来源声明总数与返回标识不一致\"). Good.\n\nTermination condition: \n```\nif not page_ids:\n    if len(pmids) != count: incomplete\n    else: break → complete/no_records\noffset += len(page_ids)\nif len(pmids) >= count: break  (then verify)\n```\nCareful: if len(pmids) >= count after appending, we can stop. Then verify len(pmids) == count else incomplete (count drift). \n\nLet me write it as a loop with max_pages:\n\n```python\noffset = 0\ntotal: int | None = None\nwhile True:\n    if page_number > max_pages: return finish(\"incomplete\", \"页数预算已耗尽；保留部分证据并等待恢复\")\n    remaining = min(max_page_bytes, max_total_bytes - byte_count)\n    if remaining <= 0: return finish(\"incomplete\", \"获取字节预算已耗尽；必须恢复，不能认定检索完成\")\n    url = build_pubmed_term_search_url(term, retstart=offset, retmax=page_size)\n    try:\n        page = _esearch_page(download, url, timeout, remaining)  # raises typed errors\n    except PubMedFetchError as error:\n        return finish(error.status, str(error))\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, better: map exception → status via a method on the exception: `status: ClassVar[FetchStatus]`. Let me give each exception a `status` attribute:\n\n```python\nclass PubMedFetchError(RuntimeError):\n    status: FetchStatus = \"invalid_response\"\nclass PubMedTimeoutError(PubMedFetchError): status = \"network_error\"\nclass PubMedNetworkError(PubMedFetchError): status = \"network_error\"\nclass PubMedRateLimitedError(PubMedFetchError): status = \"rate_limited\"\nclass PubMedHttpError(PubMedFetchError): status = \"access_denied\" ... \n```\nHmm, HTTP error status varies (401/403 → access_denied, 5xx → network_error). So PubMedHttpError carries `.code` and the mapping function decides. Let me do:\n\n```python\nclass PubMedHttpError(PubMedFetchError):\n    def __init__(self, code: int) -> None:\n        super().__init__(f\"来源返回HTTP {code}\")\n        self.code = code\n    @property\n    def status(self) -> FetchStatus: ...\n```\nHmm, mixing class attr and property is awkward. Simpler: a module function `_status_for(error: PubMedFetchError) -> FetchStatus` with isinstance checks. But \"各自抛不同异常类型\" is then the discriminator — fine and explicit. Hmm, but I want the receipt to carry the distinct status.\n\nActually, simplest and most readable:\n\n```python\nclass PubMedFetchError(RuntimeError):\n    \"\"\"PubMed 获取失败的共同基类；每种失败都有独立类型和状态。\"\"\"\n    status: FetchStatus = \"invalid_response\"\n    def __init__(self, diagnostic: str) -> None:\n        super().__init__(diagnostic)\n        self.diagnostic = diagnostic\n\nclass PubMedTimeoutError(PubMedFetchError):\n    status: FetchStatus = \"network_error\"\nclass PubMedRateLimitedError(PubMedFetchError):\n    status: FetchStatus = \"rate_limited\"\nclass PubMedAccessDeniedError(PubMedFetchError):\n    status: FetchStatus = \"access_denied\"\nclass PubMedServerError(PubMedFetchError):\n    status: FetchStatus = \"network_error\"\nclass PubMedMalformedResponseError(PubMedFetchError):\n    status: FetchStatus = \"invalid_response\"\nclass PubMedEmptyResultError(PubMedFetchError):\n    status: FetchStatus = \"no_records\"\n```\nCareful: `status` as a class attribute annotated `FetchStatus` — mypy strict is fine with ClassVar. Use `ClassVar[FetchStatus]`. Hmm, mypy strict + pydantic? These are plain classes, fine.\n\nWait: `PubMedEmptyResultError` with status \"no_records\" — the orchestrator must NOT catch it as a failure. Hmm. Actually: is \"空结果\"