": \"0\", \"idlist\": [\"1\",\"2\"], \"translationset\": [], \"querytranslation\": \"...\"}}\n```\nNote count/retmax/retstart are **strings** in the JSON! Yes, NCBI returns them as strings. So parse with `int(value)` after validating `str.isdigit()`. Careful: esearch JSON may include `\"ERROR\"` key when the term is bad → `esearchresult.ERROR` → malformed/invalid_response.\n\nValidation:\n- payload is dict, `esearchresult` is dict\n- no \"ERROR\" key\n- count: str or int, nonneg int\n- retstart: == offset (echo) — hmm, must I? NCBI echoes retstart. If missing... Let me require retstart present and equal to the requested offset, else invalid_response. This is also the \"cursor\" honesty check. Hmm, is it too strict for real-world? NCBI does return retstart. I'll require it.\n\nHmm, but with the retstart echo check, the \"重复游标\" test can be built by returning a page whose retstart equals the requested one but with the same idlist. My `new_ids` guard catches that. Good.\n\n- retmax: present, positive int? Just check it's an int ≥ 0. Hmm, don't over-validate. Let me require retmax to be a nonneg int and ≤ page_size... no, skip.\n- idlist: list of numeric strings.\n\nAlso guard: len(idlist) must be ≤ retmax? Skip.\n\n- `querytranslation`? Not needed.\n\n### efetch XML validation\n\n- content-type must be application/xml or text/xml → else invalid_response.\n- parse with parse_pubmed_efetch_xml → ValueError → PubMedMalformedResponseError.\n- Check: if the response contains `<ERROR>` (E-utilities returns `<ERROR>...</ERROR>` with 200 OK!) → must be caught. parse_pubmed_efetch_xml would return () since no PubmedArticle → then my \"missing PMIDs\" check → incomplete. Hmm, better to detect `<ERROR>` explicitly → invalid_response. Let me add: `if b\"<ERROR>\" in body` → hmm, byte-level. Better: check the parsed root? parse_pubmed_efetch_xml doesn't expose the root. I'll check the decoded text for `<ERROR>` — cheap and honest. Actually a clean way: `if \"<ERROR>\" in body.decode(\"utf-8\", \"replace\")`. Hmm, XML could be `<ERROR>` at root: `<eFetchResult><ERROR>Invalid uid</ERROR></eFetchResult>`. Yes. I'll check.\n\nActually also esearch: the JSON error is handled via the ERROR key.\n\n- requested PMIDs vs returned records: sets must be equal, else incomplete (\"来源未返回全部请求记录\").\n- duplicates within returned records → incomplete? parse would produce two records with same pmid → detect via len(set) != len(records) → incomplete.\n\n### efetch batching\n\n`efetch_batch_size: int = 200`. Build URL:\n```\nhttps://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/efetch.fcgi?db=pubmed&retmode=xml&rettype=abstract&id=1,2,3\n```\nURL length: 200 PMIDs × 9 chars = 1800 + base. Fine.\n\nHmm — should efetch reuse `build_pubmed_nct_search_url` too? No, it's a different endpoint (efetch.fcgi). I'll derive the efetch endpoint from the esearch endpoint by replacing the path's last segment? `esearch.fcgi` → `efetch.fcgi`. That's a reasonable single-source derivation: \n\n```python\n_EFETCH_ENDPOINT = _ESEARCH_ENDPOINT.replace(\"esearch.fcgi\", \"efetch.fcgi\")\n```\nHmm, `.replace` is stringly. Or `urlsplit` + `path.rsplit(\"/\", 1)[0] + \"/efetch.fcgi\"`. That's cleaner:\n\n```python\n_BASE_URL, _SEARCH_PATH = ... \n```\nHmm. I think it's acceptable and honest: the E-utilities host is single-sourced from pubmed.py. Let me write:\n\n```python\n_ESEARCH = urlsplit(build_pubmed_nct_search_url(\"NCT00000001\"))\n_EUTILS_ROOT = urlunsplit((_ESEARCH.scheme, _ESEARCH.netloc, _ESEARCH.path.rsplit(\"/\", 1)[0], \"\", \"\"))\n# _EUTILS_ROOT = \"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils\"\ndef _esearch_url(...) -> f\"{_EUTILS_ROOT}/esearch.fcgi?{urlencode(params)}\"\ndef _efetch_url(...) -> f\"{_EUTILS_ROOT}/efetch.fcgi?{urlencode(params)}\"\n```\nAnd params for esearch reuse `parse_qs(_ESEARCH.query)` for db/retmode. Hmm, `retmode=json` for esearch, and efetch uses retmode=xml. So sharing retmode is wrong for efetch. Only share `db=pubmed`. \n\nSo:\n```python\n_SEARCH_DEFAULTS = {key: values[0] for key, values in parse_qs(_ESEARCH.query).items() if key != \"term\"}\n# {\"db\": \"pubmed\", \"retmode\": \"json\", \"retmax\": \"10000\"}\n```\nThen esearch params = {**_SEARCH_DEFAULTS without retmax, \"term\":..., \"retstart\":..., \"retmax\":...}. Hmm, retmax=10000 from the base would be overridden. Let me explicitly build:\n\n```python\ndef build_pubmed_term_search_url(term: str, *, retstart: int, retmax: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Term 检索 URL：端点与 db/retmode 复用 pubmed.py 的规范构造，仅替换检索式与分页。\"\"\"\n    defaults = {key: values[0] for key, values in parse_qs(_ESEARCH.query).items()}\n    defaults.pop(\"term\")\n    params = {**defaults, \"term\": term, \"retstart\": str(retstart), \"retmax\": str(retmax)}\n    return f\"{_EUTILS_ROOT}/esearch.fcgi?{urlencode(params)}\"\n```\nOrder: dict preserves insertion; defaults has db, retmode, retmax → then overridden retmax stays at position 3. urlencode order: db, retmode, retmax, term, retstart. Fine (deterministic).\n\nHmm, `defaults.pop(\"term\")` — the base always has \"term\". OK.\n\nThis is genuinely \"复用 pubmed.py 的 URL 构造\": the endpoint host/path, db, retmode all come from `build_pubmed_nct_search_url`. And it means if pubmed.py's endpoint changes, this follows. I'll document it.\n\nIs the module-level `_ESEARCH = urlsplit(build_pubmed_nct_search_url(\"NCT00000001\"))` acceptable? It's a constant computed at import. It can't fail (valid NCT). OK.\n\nHmm, wait. `urlunsplit((scheme, netloc, path.rsplit(\"/\",1)[0], \"\", \"\"))` — path is \"/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi\" → rsplit(\"/\",1)[0] = \"/entrez/eutils\" → urlunsplit gives \"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils\". Good.\n\n### Transport protocol\n\nMirror ctgov:\n```python\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass HttpPage:\n    status: int\n    content_type: str\n    body: bytes\n    final_url: str\n\nclass Transport(Protocol):\n    def __call__(self, url: str, timeout: float, max_bytes: int) -> HttpPage: ...\n```\nTests monkeypatch `pubmed_fetch._download`.\n\nHmm, but the task says \"测试全部用合成 transport（monkeypatch urllib/opener）\". Passing `transport=` is the ctgov pattern; I'll do that (and the CLI test monkeypatches `_download`).\n\n### Content type checks\nesearch: `application/json` — NCBI returns `application/json` for retmode=json. Good.\nefetch: `text/xml` (NCBI returns `text/xml` for efetch XML? Actually it returns \"text/xml\" or \"application/xml\"). Accept both. The `_download` uses `response.headers.get_content_type()` which normalizes \"text/xml; charset=UTF-8\" → \"text/xml\". Accept {\"application/xml\", \"text/xml\"}.\n\n### Download / redirect\nCopy ctgov's `_NoRedirect` + `_download`, but raise typed errors:\n\n```python\ndef _download(url: str, timeout: float, max_bytes: int) -> HttpPage:\n    request = Request(url, headers={\"Accept\": \"application/json, application/xml, text/xml\", \"User-Agent\": \"CI-Workflow/1.4 public-research\"})\n    try:\n        with build_opener(_NoRedirect()).open(request, timeout=timeout) as response:\n            return HttpPage(response.status, response.headers.get_content_type(), response.read(max_bytes + 1), response.geturl())\n    except HTTPError as error:\n        code = error.code\n        error.close()\n        if code == 429: raise PubMedRateLimitedError(...)\n        if code in {401,403}: raise PubMedAccessDeniedError(...)\n        if code >= 500: raise PubMedServerError(...)\n        raise PubMedHttpError(...)   # other HTTP error\n    except TimeoutError as error: raise PubMedTimeoutError(...)\n    except (OSError, URLError) as error: raise PubMedNetworkError(...)\n```\nNote: `URLError` is a subclass of OSError, and TimeoutError is a subclass of OSError too (OSError's subclass? `TimeoutError` is a builtin exception, and `socket.timeout` is an alias of `TimeoutError` in 3.10+; `TimeoutError` IS a subclass of OSError). So order matters: TimeoutError before OSError. And URLError before OSError. Also urllib wraps socket timeout in URLError sometimes (`URLError(reason=TimeoutError())`). Handle: \n\n```python\nexcept URLError as error:\n    if isinstance(error.reason, TimeoutError): raise PubMedTimeoutError(...)\n    raise PubMedNetworkError(...)\nexcept TimeoutError: raise PubMedTimeoutError(...)\nexcept OSError: raise PubMedNetworkError(...)\n```\nHmm, but ctgov's test passes `TimeoutError()` raised by the transport — and ctgov's own `_download` isn't used in those tests (transport replaces it). In my tests, the synthetic transport will raise `TimeoutError()` directly, which means `fetch_pubmed_results` must catch `TimeoutError` from the transport too. Since my `_download` converts, but a custom transport raises raw exceptions... \n\nHmm! In ctgov, `fetch_ctgov_condition` catches `(OSError, URLError, TimeoutError)` around `download(url, ...)`. So the orchestrator also handles raw transport exceptions. I should do the same: wrap the download call in try/except that converts raw OSError/URLError/TimeoutError → typed errors. That makes both the real `_download` and synthetic transports consistent.\n\nSo: `_download` raises typed errors AND the orchestrator catches `(OSError, URLError, TimeoutError)` → maps to network_error/network_error. Hmm, but then the distinct type for timeout at the orchestrator boundary is lost... \n\nCleaner: `_download` does NOT convert; it just returns HttpPage for HTTPError (like ctgov) — no wait, then 429 handling is in the orchestrator via `response.status == 429`, like ctgov. Hmm, but then where do the typed exceptions get raised?\n\nOK here's the cleanest architecture:\n1. `_download(url, timeout, max_bytes) -> HttpPage`: stdlib, no redirect, raises `PubMedTimeoutError` / `PubMedNetworkError` for transport failures (typed), and raises `PubMedHttpError`-family for HTTP error codes (401/403/429/5xx/other). Returns HttpPage only for 2xx.\n\nHmm, but ctgov returns HttpPage for any HTTPError status so the orchestrator can classify. If `_download` raises typed HTTP errors, the orchestrator catches `PubMedFetchError` and uses `error.status`. That's clean! And the status ClassVar approach makes it work.\n\n2. `fetch_pubmed_results` catches `PubMedFetchError` → `finish(error.status, error.diagnostic)`; and also catches raw `(OSError, URLError, TimeoutError)` from *custom transports* → finish(\"network_error\", ...). Hmm, but that would map a raw TimeoutError to network_error, losing the timeout distinction. Since the status for timeout is also \"network_error\" in ctgov's taxonomy, that's fine. But then \"超时\" and \"网络错误\" have the same status — acceptable (ctgov does the same). The distinct *exception types* still exist.\n\nHmm, wait. But if the synthetic transport raises TimeoutError and the orchestrator catches raw OSError→network_error, the test can't distinguish. My tests will cover:\n- `_download`-level: monkeypatch `build_opener`? That's complex. Hmm, \"monkeypatch urllib/opener\" is explicitly requested! \"全部合成 transport（monkeypatch urllib/opener）\".\n\nSo one test should monkeypatch `urllib.request.build_opener` (as imported in pubmed_fetch) to return a fake opener whose `.open()` raises `URLError(socket.timeout())` / raises HTTPError(429) — verifying the real `_download` converts to typed exceptions. And other tests use `transport=` synthetic pages. That covers \"monkeypatch urllib/opener\" and the \"各自抛不同异常类型\" requirement at the real boundary.\n\nGood. So:\n\n```python\ndef _download(url, timeout, max_bytes) -> HttpPage:\n    ...raises PubMedFetchError subclasses...\n```\n\nAnd in the orchestrator:\n```python\ntry:\n    response = download(url, timeout, remaining)\nexcept PubMedFetchError as error:\n    return finish(error.status, error.diagnostic)\nexcept (OSError, URLError, TimeoutError):\n    return finish(\"network_error\", \"公开接口网络获取失败；不是无数据\")\n```\nNote: URLError is a subclass of OSError, and TimeoutError is a subclass of OSError; ruff might flag redundant exception tuples? No, ruff doesn't check that. But mypy is fine.\n\nHmm, `except (OSError, URLError, TimeoutError)` — URLError and TimeoutError are subclasses of OSError so the tuple is redundant; ruff B014? B014 is \"redundant exception types in a tuple\" — hmm, B014 flags `except (Exception, TypeError)`. Does it flag OSError subclasses? B014 checks for redundant exception *handlers* based on known subclass relationships. `TimeoutError` IS a subclass of OSError, and ruff's B014 does check builtin hierarchy for OSError subclasses? Let me check: B014 \"Redundant exception types\" — it flags cases like `except (OSError, IOError)` and `except (Exception, TypeError)`. It does have a builtin mapping including OSError subclasses. To be safe, just catch `OSError` (covers URLError and TimeoutError). ctgov uses `(OSError, URLError, TimeoutError)` and presumably passes ruff... let me verify by running ruff on ctgov_fetch.py. Actually ctgov_fetch.py is new (untracked) — let me just run ruff check on it to see if the repo's ruff config flags that tuple.\n\nLet me run the verification commands on ctgov_fetch.py as a baseline to make sure ruff passes and to learn the config behavior.\n\nActually simpler: in my code I'll catch `OSError` only, with a comment. Cleaner and no ruff risk.\n\nHmm, but a synthetic transport raising `TimeoutError()` → caught by `except OSError` → network_error. Fine.\n\n### Now the receipt fields\n\nThe task: \"回执含：query、总数、条数、原始字节 sha256、CAS 相对路径、获取时刻 UTC、universe_closed、temporal_scope\".\n\nMy `PubMedFetchResult` (pydantic, frozen, extra=forbid):\n- query: str\n- status: FetchStatus\n- pagination_complete: bool\n- universe_closed: Literal[False] = False\n- temporal_scope: Literal[\"current_records\"] = \"current_records\"\n- total_count: int | None\n- records: tuple[PubMedRecord, ...]  → 条数 = len(records)\n- pages: tuple[CapturedPage, ...]    → esearch pages, each with raw_asset (sha256 + relative_path) + acquired_at\n- records_asset: ContentBlob | None  → efetch XML CAS pointer... but with batching there are multiple. So `record_pages: tuple[CapturedPage, ...]`? Hmm.\n\nLet me define a single page type for both phases:\n\n```python\nclass CapturedResponse(BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\", frozen=True)\n    request_url: str\n    acquired_at: datetime\n    raw_asset: ContentBlob\n    pmids: tuple[str, ...]        # esearch page: returned ids; efetch page: requested ids\n```\nand \n```python\nclass PubMedFetchResult(BaseModel):\n    query: str\n    status: FetchStatus\n    pagination_complete: bool\n    universe_closed: Literal[False] = False\n    temporal_scope: Literal[\"current_records\"] = \"current_records\"\n    total_count: int | None\n    records: tuple[PubMedRecord, ...]\n    search_pages: tuple[CapturedResponse, ...]\n    record_pages: tuple[CapturedResponse, ...]\n    diagnostic: str\n```\nHmm, \"原始字节 sha256、CAS 相对路径、获取时刻 UTC\" — all present via raw_asset + acquired_at.\n\nHmm, maybe simpler: one `pages` tuple covering all captured responses in order, each with `phase: Literal[\"esearch\",\"efetch\"]`. Hmm. Two tuples is clearer for the CLI receipt. Let me use one tuple with a phase field to keep the receipt flat? I'll go with two named tuples — explicit.\n\nHmm, ctgov names it `pages`. Let me name them `search_pages` and `record_pages`. OK.\n\nAlso \"条数\": maybe add an explicit `record_count: int` property? A computed property on a frozen pydantic model is fine, but `model_dump` won't include it. Hmm. The CLI will print `\"records\": len(result.records)`. And the receipt JSON (saved to CAS by CLI) will include acquisition.model_dump(exclude={\"records\"}) → then \"records\": entries. Hmm.\n\nLet me keep it simple: `records` tuple; the CLI writes the count explicitly in the pointer JSON.\n\nHmm, should `records` be excluded from the dump? ctgov excludes `studies` from the acquisition dump and writes a projection. For pubmed, the CLI should \"输出精简 JSON 指针，不回显正文\" — so the receipt JSON should exclude the full abstracts too. Let me have the CLI build:\n\n```python\nreceipt = {\n  \"schema_version\": \"1.0\",\n  \"acquisition\": result.model_dump(mode=\"json\", exclude={\"records\"}),\n  \"records\": [{\"pmid\":..., \"title\":..., \"publication_types\": [...]}, ...],   # hmm, exclude abstract?\n  \"limitation\": \"...\"\n}\n```\nHmm, \"不回显正文\" means the CLI stdout shouldn't print the text; the receipt file can contain it (it's in CAS anyway). ctgov writes content_text into per-record CAS captures. For pubmed, the raw XML is in CAS and parsed records are derivable. To keep it bounded and simple: the CLI writes a receipt JSON containing the acquisition dump (excluding records) plus per-record identity (pmid, title) — not the abstract. Hmm, but then the receipt loses the abstract... which is fine since raw XML is preserved and the parser is deterministic. Actually, ctgov's CLI does write content_text for each record (a derived slice). For pubmed, the analog would be writing the record text... \n\nSimplicity: write the receipt with `records` as `[{pmid, title, publication_types}]` and note that full text is derivable from the CAS-preserved XML. Hmm, but \"原始响应字节写入 CAS\" is the evidence anchor; the parse is deterministic from it. I think identity-only is defensible and keeps the receipt small. Hmm, but a reviewer might want the record payload... The task doesn't require it. Keep identity-only? \n\nHmm, actually — \"不回显正文\" only constrains stdout. Writing the full records into the receipt blob is harmless and more useful (and matches ctgov's approach of capturing per-record content). But abstracts can be large; the receipt would be big. ctgov's approach: each record gets its own CAS blob with content_text. I'll do the same: for each record, write a JSON blob {pmid, title, abstract, publication_types, raw_xml_asset: {...}} to CAS, and reference its capture_path+sha256 in the receipt. That mirrors ctgov's derive step nicely and gives per-record evidence pointers.\n\nHmm, is that over-engineering? The task's receipt requirement mentions only sha256/relative path of raw bytes. Let me keep the CLI receipt simpler: records with pmid/title/publication_types + the raw XML asset pointer. Hmm.\n\nDecision: CLI writes:\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_version\": \"1.0\",\n  \"acquisition\": {query, status, pagination_complete, universe_closed, temporal_scope, total_count, search_pages, record_pages, diagnostic},\n  \"record_count\": N,\n  \"records\": [{\"pmid\",\"title\",\"publication_types\"}],\n  \"limitation\": \"仅获取PubMed当前记录；论文-试验关系判定、闭包及独立科学复核尚未完成\"\n}\n```\nand stdout prints a compact pointer:\n```json\n{\"capture_path\",\"sha256\",\"status\",\"pagination_complete\",\"records\":N,\"total_count\":T,\"universe_closed\":false}\n```\nExit code: 0 if pagination_complete else 7. (Align with ctgov: `return 0 if result.pagination_complete and projection == \"complete\" else 7`.) For pubmed, `pagination_complete` is True for complete/no_records. So `return 0 if result.pagination_complete else 7`.\n\nHmm, ctgov CLI also verifies the project workspace (`verify_project_workspace(root)`) and raises ContractError on failure. The task says `--root <项目或输出目录>`. Hmm — \"项目或输出目录\". fetch-ctgov requires a real project workspace. For alignment, I'll require a project workspace too (verify_project_workspace), since ContentAddressedStore needs a project root. Hmm, but \"输出目录\"... The delegated spec says \"--root <项目或输出目录>\". If I call verify_project_workspace on a plain output dir, it fails. \n\nHmm. ctgov's CLI: `verify_project_workspace(root)` then fetch. The task says \"行为对齐 fetch-ctgov\". I'll do the same (project workspace required) and document that `--root` must be an initialized project (like fetch-ctgov) — noting the deviation from the literal \"项目或输出目录\" in the report. Hmm, is that the right call? The `--root` arg also accepts `--project` alias in ctgov. I'll mirror: `research_fetch_pubmed.add_argument(\"--root\", \"--project\", dest=\"root\", required=True)`.\n\nHmm, but \"输出目录\"... If the dispatcher meant a plain directory, requiring a project would break their expectation. But ContentAddressedStore(project_root) works on any dir — `verify_project_workspace` is the only blocker. Risk assessment: aligning with fetch-ctgov (which the prompt explicitly says) is safer and consistent; a plain output dir would produce a project-less CAS store which violates the project's evidence model. Also the CLI's other handlers verify the workspace. Go with verify_project_workspace.\n\nWait, let me check what verify_project_workspace does — it may require project.yaml with a contract. Let me read it quickly.\n\n### Tests to write\n\nFile: tests/integration/sources/test_pubmed_fetch.py\n\nTests:\n1. `test_two_page_search_preserves_raw_bytes_and_pagination` — synthetic Pages transport returning esearch JSON page 1 (count=2, retstart=0, retmax=1, idlist=[\"111\"]) and page 2 (count=2, retstart=1, retmax=1, idlist=[\"222\"]), then efetch XML with both records. Assert: status complete, pagination_complete, total_count=2, 2 records, search_pages[0..1] URLs contain retstart=0/1 and retmax=1, CAS bytes match sha256, `universe_closed is False`, `temporal_scope == \"current_records\"`, acquired_at UTC.\n2. `test_rate_limit_is_distinct_from_empty` — 429 → status rate_limited, no records, pagination_complete False, and exception type raised by `_download`... hmm. For the 429 through a synthetic transport returning HttpPage(429,...) → orchestrator sees status 429. But wait, in my design `_download` raises for 429. If the synthetic transport *returns* HttpPage(429, ...), the orchestrator must classify it. So the orchestrator must ALSO check `response.status` for non-200 codes (like ctgov). Yes — do both: check response.status in the orchestrator (handles synthetic transports) and have `_download` raise typed errors (real path).\n\nHmm, that's duplicated classification logic. Alternative: `_download` returns HttpPage for any status (like ctgov) and a single `_classify_response(response)` helper raises the typed errors, called by the orchestrator. Then `_download` only converts transport failures (timeout/OSError) to typed errors. That's clean:\n\n```python\ndef _download(url, timeout, max_bytes) -> HttpPage:\n    \"\"\"... raises PubMedTimeoutError/PubMedNetworkError; HTTP status is classified by caller.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        ...\n    except HTTPError as error:\n        code = error.code\n        error.close()\n        return HttpPage(code, \"\", b\"\", url)   # never persist an error page\n    except URLError as error:\n        if isinstance(error.reason, TimeoutError): raise PubMedTimeoutError(...)\n        raise PubMedNetworkError(...)\n    except TimeoutError as error: raise PubMedTimeoutError(...)\n    except OSError as error: raise PubMedNetworkError(...)\n```\nand\n\n```python\ndef _classify_http(status: int, *, context: str) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Raise the distinct typed error for a non-200 status.\"\"\"\n    if status == 429: raise PubMedRateLimitedError(...)\n    if status in {401, 403}: raise PubMedAccessDeniedError(...)\n    if status >= 500: raise PubMedServerError(...)\n    raise PubMedHttpError(...)\n```\nHmm, but for `response.status >= 400` and non-200 statuses like 302 (redirect) → invalid_response in ctgov. Let me map:\n- 429 → rate_limited\n- 401/403 → access_denied\n- >=500 → network_error (server)\n- other non-200 (incl. 3xx) → invalid_response\n\nSo `PubMedHttpError` base → invalid_response; `PubMedRateLimitedError` → rate_limited; `PubMedAccessDeniedError` → access_denied; `PubMedServerError` → network_error.\n\nAlso final_url != url → invalid_response (redirect detected even if status 200) → `PubMedMalformedResponseError`.\n\nOK. And the orchestrator calls `_classify_http(response.status, ...)` in a try/except PubMedFetchError. Good — and the `_download`-level HTTPError path returns an HttpPage which the orchestrator then classifies. \n\nThen for the \"monkeypatch opener\" test: I can monkeypatch `pubmed_fetch.build_opener` to