{"type":"assistant","uuid":"01a08f76-9680-7bf2-a948-b6a89b5069c9","session_id":"01a08f74-c077-7bd3-8de8-2cfab5bccb52","message":{"id":"01a08f7696807bf2a948b6a62bd2137d","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good. `verify_project_workspace` requires a real project. I'll require it (aligned with fetch-ctgov).\n\nNow let me create the task list and start writing. First the test file (TDD: write tests, see them fail, then implement).\n\nLet me create tasks.\n\nNow, the test file design. Synthetic transport pattern:\n\n```python\nclass Routes:\n    \"\"\"Synthetic transport: 按 URL 特征返回预置响应。\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, responses): ...\n    def __call__(self, url, timeout, max_bytes) -> HttpPage: ...\n```\n\nSimpler: a `Transport` that maps by \"which phase\" — since the orchestrator calls esearch pages in order and then efetch batches in order. Let me use a queue-based fake that returns queued responses, recording URLs:\n\n```python\nclass Responses:\n    def __init__(self, items: list[HttpPage | Exception]) -> None:\n        self.items = iter(items)\n        self.urls: list[str] = []\n    def __call__(self, url, timeout, max_bytes):\n        self.urls.append(url)\n        value = next(self.items)\n        if isinstance(value, Exception): raise value\n        return value\n```\nBut HttpPage is a frozen dataclass; constructing `HttpPage(200, \"application/json\", body, url)` requires knowing the url... In ctgov's test, `Pages.__call__` builds the page with the actual url. I'll do similar with helper builders:\n\n```python\ndef _json_page(payload: dict[str, object]) -> bytes: return json.dumps(payload).encode()\n\nclass Route:\n    def __init__(self, *items): ...\n```\nLet me define a fake transport that dispatches on URL path:\n\n```python\nclass FakeEutils:\n    \"\"\"Synthetic E-utilities: 只按 URL 返回预置响应，绝不联网。\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, search: list[object], fetch: list[object]) -> None:\n        self.search = iter(search); self.fetch = iter(fetch); self.urls: list[str] = []\n    def __call__(self, url, timeout, max_bytes):\n        self.urls.append(url)\n        queue = self.fetch if \"efetch.fcgi\" in url else self.search\n        value = next(queue)\n        if isinstance(value, Exception): raise value\n        if isinstance(value, bytes): return HttpPage(200, \"application/json\" if \"esearch\" in url else \"text/xml\", value, url)\n        return value  # HttpPage\n```\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me simplify: since search pages come first then fetch pages, a single queue in call order is simplest and mirrors ctgov's `Pages`. Tests that need multiple phases just queue them in order. Good — simple, and the URLs are recorded for assertions.\n\nBut for the retry/loop tests I need the same page repeatedly. Queue with repeated entries is fine.\n\nI'll write:\n```python\nclass Pages:\n    def __init__(self, *items: HttpPage | Exception) -> None: ...\n```\nHmm, ctgov's Pages takes a list and builds HttpPage itself from a payload dict. For pubmed I need both JSON and XML. Let me make the queue hold `bytes | Exception | HttpPage`:\n- bytes → 200 with the content type inferred from the URL (esearch → application/json, efetch → text/xml)\n- HttpPage → returned as-is\n- Exception → raised\n\nThat's flexible and compact.\n\n```python\nclass Pages:\n    def __init__(self, items: list[bytes | HttpPage | Exception]) -> None:\n        self.items = iter(items)\n        self.urls: list[str] = []\n\n    def __call__(self, url: str, timeout: float, max_bytes: int) -> HttpPage:\n        self.urls.append(url)\n        value = next(self.items)\n        if isinstance(value, Exception):\n            raise value\n        if isinstance(value, HttpPage):\n            return value\n        media = \"application/json\" if \"esearch.fcgi\" in url else \"text/xml\"\n        return HttpPage(200, media, value, url)\n```\n\nThen XML builders:\n```python\ndef _article(pmid: str) -> str:\n    return (\n        \"<PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation>\"\n        f\"<PMID>{pmid}</PMID>\"\n        \"<Article><ArticleTitle>合成题名</ArticleTitle>\"\n        \"<Abstract><AbstractText>合成摘要</AbstractText></Abstract>\"\n        \"<PublicationTypeList><PublicationType>Randomized Controlled Trial</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList>\"\n        \"</Article></MedlineCitation></PubmedArticle>\"\n    )\n\ndef _efetch_xml(*pmids: str) -> bytes:\n    return (\"<?xml version=\\\"1.0\\\"?><PubmedArticleSet>\" + \"\".join(_article(p) for p in pmids) + \"</PubmedArticleSet>\").encode()\n```\n\nesearch JSON builder:\n```python\ndef _esearch_payload(count: int, retstart: int, retmax: int, ids: list[str]) -> bytes:\n    return json.dumps({\"header\": {\"type\": \"esearch\"}, \"esearchresult\": {\n        \"count\": str(count), \"retstart\": str(retstart), \"retmax\": str(retmax), \"idlist\": ids,\n    }}).encode()\n```\n\nNow tests:\n\n1. `test_two_page_search_then_fetch_preserves_raw_bytes_and_cursor`\n```python\npages = Pages([\n    _esearch_payload(2, 0, 1, [\"111\"]),\n    _esearch_payload(2, 1, 1, [\"222\"]),\n    _efetch_xml(\"111\", \"222\"),\n])\nresult = fetch_pubmed_results(tmp_path, \"pembrolizumab AND NSCLC\", page_size=1, transport=pages)\nassert result.status == \"complete\"\nassert result.pagination_complete\nassert result.total_count == 2\nassert [r.pmid for r in result.records] == [\"111\", \"222\"]\nassert result.universe_closed is False\nassert result.temporal_scope == \"current_records\"\nassert result.acquired_at... hmm, acquired_at is per-page. \n```\nTask requires \"获取时刻 UTC\" in the receipt. Per-page acquired_at is UTC (from clock). Let me also add a top-level `acquired_at: datetime` to the result — hmm, is that needed? \"回执含：... 获取时刻 UTC\". The pages have acquired_at, but a top-level one is friendlier. I'll add `acquired_at: datetime` (clock at start? or at completion?). Let me use the clock() call at the moment the first page is acquired... Simpler: `acquired_at` = the clock value when the receipt is finalized (finish()). Hmm, but `finish()` is called on failure paths too. Let me just record `acquired_at = clock()` once at the beginning... Hmm, \"获取时刻\" = acquisition time. I'll capture it at the start of the run: `started_at = clock()` and validate tz. Hmm, ctgov validates tz per page. I'll validate once at the start and use it for the receipt, and each page gets its own `clock()` (already validated by the same check).\n\nSimplest: validate tz once at the top: `acquired_at = _utc_now(clock)`; use it for the receipt; pages also use their own clock() calls... that could differ. Hmm. Keep it simple: `acquired_at` on the result = the moment the receipt is finished, via clock() in finish() — but then tz validation happens in finish. Let me do: at the start, `started_at = clock()`; validate tz immediately. In finish(): `acquired_at=clock()`? That means two clock calls.\n\nCleanest: pages capture their own acquired_at (like ctgov), and the result has `acquired_at` = the first page's acquired_at (or the start time if no pages). Let me do:\n\n```python\nacquired_at = _require_aware(clock())\n```\nonce at the top, used for the result, and each page uses `_require_aware(clock())`. Two clock calls per run, harmless. Hmm, the test clock is a lambda returning a fixed datetime → deterministic. Fine.\n\nHmm, but ctgov's clock is `Callable[[], datetime]` and the derive function replays with a clock reading from pages. Fine.\n\nLet me keep: `started_at = _require_aware(clock())` at top; pages use `_require_aware(clock())`.\n\nHmm, \"获取时刻 UTC\" — validate `tzinfo is not None and utcoffset() is not None`. ctgov raises ValueError. Same.\n\nThen assert `result.acquired_at.tzinfo is not None` and `result.acquired_at.utcoffset() == timedelta(0)`? Only if the clock returns UTC. Default clock uses datetime.now(UTC). Test uses a fixed UTC datetime → assert `result.acquired_at == datetime(2026,9,11,3,0,tzinfo=UTC)`. Good.\n\nCAS verification: \n```python\nstore = ContentAddressedStore(tmp_path)\nfor page in (*result.search_pages, *result.record_pages):\n    raw = store.read_bytes(page.raw_asset)\n    assert hashlib.sha256(raw).hexdigest() == page.raw_asset.sha256\n    assert page.raw_asset.relative_path == f\"evidence/raw/sha256/{sha[:2]}/{sha}.bin\"\n```\nAnd assert the bytes equal the exact synthetic bytes.\n\n2. `test_search_cursor_is_echoed_and_never_reused` — retstart/retmax in URLs:\n```python\nassert parse_qs(urlsplit(pages.urls[0]).query)[\"retstart\"] == [\"0\"]\nassert parse_qs(urlsplit(pages.urls[1]).query)[\"retstart\"] == [\"1\"]\nassert parse_qs(urlsplit(pages.urls[1]).query)[\"retmax\"] == [\"1\"]\nassert parse_qs(urlsplit(pages.urls[0]).query)[\"term\"] == [\"合成检索式\"]\n```\nCould merge into test 1. Keep separate for clarity? Merge to keep the file lean. I'll assert inside test 1.\n\n3. `test_repeated_page_is_not_completion` (重复游标死循环保护):\n```python\npages = Pages([\n    _esearch_payload(4, 0, 1, [\"111\"]),\n    _esearch_payload(4, 1, 1, [\"111\"]),   # same id again\n])\nresult = fetch_pubmed_results(tmp_path, \"term\", page_size=1, transport=pages)\nassert result.status == \"incomplete\"\nassert not result.pagination_complete\nassert len(result.search_pages) == 2  # partial evidence retained\n```\nAnd ensure it doesn't loop forever — the test finishing proves it. Also test a page that repeats the same retstart with the same idlist: covered.\n\nHmm, also add: a page whose retstart doesn't echo the request → invalid_response:\n```python\npages = Pages([_esearch_payload(2, 0, 1, [\"111\"]), _esearch_payload(2, 0, 1, [\"222\"])])\nresult = ...\nassert result.status == \"invalid_response\"\n```\nGood — that's the \"cursor mismatch\" guard. Hmm, is requiring the echo too strict? It's a deliberate honesty check. I'll include it as a test case.\n\n4. `test_failure_kinds_are_distinct_and_never_empty_results`:\n```python\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"failure,expected\", [\n    (TimeoutError(), \"network_error\"),\n    (URLError(\"connection refused\"), \"network_error\"),\n    (HttpPage(429, \"\", b\"\", \"\"), \"rate_limited\"),\n    (HttpPage(403, \"\", b\"\", \"\"), \"access_denied\"),\n    (HttpPage(503, \"\", b\"\", \"\"), \"network_error\"),\n    (HttpPage(302, \"\", b\"\", \"\"), \"invalid_response\"),\n    (b\"<not json\", \"invalid_response\"),\n    (b'{\"esearchresult\": {\"count\": \"1\", \"retstart\": \"0\", \"retmax\": \"1\", \"idlist\": [], \"ERROR\": \"bad term\"}}', \"invalid_response\"),\n])\ndef test_failure_is_never_reported_as_no_records(...):\n    result = fetch_pubmed_results(tmp_path, \"term\", transport=Pages([failure]))\n    assert result.status == expected\n    assert not result.pagination_complete\n    assert result.records == ()\n    assert result.total_count is None\n```\nWait — HttpPage(302, \"\", b\"\", \"\") — final_url \"\" != url → invalid_response. But careful: my orchestrator checks status first (302 → invalid_response via _raise_for_status \"other\"). Either way invalid_response.\n\nHmm, `HttpPage(429, ...)` — but wait, the \"status\" for 429... `_raise_for_status(429)` raises PubMedRateLimitedError → finish(\"rate_limited\"). Good.\n\nAlso assert no CAS files written for these failures: `assert not (tmp_path / \"evidence\").exists()`.\n\n5. `test_empty_search_is_a_distinct_state`:\n```python\npages = Pages([_esearch_payload(0, 0, 200, [])])\nresult = fetch_pubmed_results(tmp_path, \"term\", transport=pages)\nassert result.status == \"no_records\"\nassert result.pagination_complete and result.total_count == 0 and result.records == ()\nassert result.universe_closed is False\nassert len(result.search_pages) == 1  # raw empty response retained as proof\nassert not result.record_pages\n```\nAlso assert that the raw bytes are readable from CAS.\n\n6. `test_bad_xml_is_not_an_empty_record_set`:\n```python\npages = Pages([_esearch_payload(1,0,1,[\"111\"]), b\"<PubmedArticleSet><broken\"])\nresult = ...\nassert result.status == \"invalid_response\"\nassert not result.pagination_complete\nassert result.records == ()\n```\nAnd a variant: well-formed XML with zero articles while 1 pmid requested → incomplete:\n```python\npages = Pages([_esearch_payload(1,0,1,[\"111\"]), _efetch_xml()])\nassert result.status == \"incomplete\"\nassert not result.pagination_complete\n```\nAnd an `<ERROR>` document → invalid_response.\n\nHmm, `_efetch_xml()` with no articles produces `<PubmedArticleSet></PubmedArticleSet>`. parse returns (). Then my orchestrator: requested {\"111\"} - returned set() → non-empty missing → incomplete. Good.\n\n7. `test_fetch_is_chunked_and_duplicate_records_are_rejected`:\nMaybe skip — keep it lean. Hmm, batching: test with efetch_batch_size=1 and 2 pmids → 2 efetch requests. That's worth one test since it exercises the batch loop. Let me include:\n```python\npages = Pages([\n    _esearch_payload(2, 0, 2, [\"111\", \"222\"]),\n    _efetch_xml(\"111\"),\n    _efetch_xml(\"222\"),\n])\nresult = fetch_pubmed_results(tmp_path, \"term\", efetch_batch_size=1, transport=pages)\nassert result.status == \"complete\"\nassert [r.pmid for r in result.records] == [\"111\", \"222\"]\nassert len(result.record_pages) == 2\nassert parse_qs(urlsplit(pages.urls[1]).query)[\"id\"] == [\"111\"]\n```\nGood.\n\n8. `test_transport_failures_raise_distinct_exception_types` — monkeypatch opener:\n```python\ndef test_real_download_raises_distinct_transport_errors(monkeypatch):\n    from ci_workflow.sources.connectors import pubmed_fetch\n    class Opener:\n        def __init__(self, error): self.error = error\n        def open(self, request, timeout): raise self.error\n    for error, expected in [\n        (URLError(TimeoutError(\"timed out\")), pubmed_fetch.PubMedTimeoutError),\n        (URLError(\"connection refused\"), pubmed_fetch.PubMedNetworkError),\n        (TimeoutError(\"timed out\"), pubmed_fetch.PubMedTimeoutError),\n        (OSError(\"broken pipe\"), pubmed_fetch.PubMedNetworkError),\n        (HTTPError(\"https://x\", 429, \"Too Many Requests\", {}, None), ...)\n    ]:\n        monkeypatch.setattr(pubmed_fetch, \"build_opener\", lambda handler, error=error: Opener(error))\n        with pytest.raises(expected): pubmed_fetch._download(\"https://eutils...\", 5, 1024)\n```\nFor HTTPError, `_download` returns HttpPage(429) (no raise) → assert status. Let me handle that as a separate assertion:\n```python\n    monkeypatch.setattr(pubmed_fetch, \"build_opener\", lambda handler: Opener(HTTPError(url, 429, \"...\", {}, None)))\n    page = pubmed_fetch._download(\"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/...\", 5.0, 1024)\n    assert page.status == 429 and page.body == b\"\"\n```\nThen classification: `with pytest.raises(pubmed_fetch.PubMedRateLimitedError): pubmed_fetch._raise_for_status(429, context=\"检索\")`. Hmm, naming a private function in tests is acceptable but let me instead assert via the public path: a transport returning HttpPage(429) → status \"rate_limited\" (already covered in test 4). \n\nI want the \"distinct exception types\" requirement evidenced. Let me add one test that asserts each distinct type is raised from the classification helper:\n\n```python\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"status,expected\", [\n    (429, PubMedRateLimitedError),\n    (403, PubMedAccessDeniedError),\n    (401, PubMedAccessDeniedError),\n    (503, PubMedServerError),\n    (302, PubMedHttpError),\n])\ndef test_http_statuses_raise_distinct_typed_errors(status, expected):\n    with pytest.raises(expected): pubmed_fetch._classify_status(status)\n```\nHmm, private API in tests. The project already does similar? test_ctgov_fetch uses `monkeypatch.setattr(ctgov_fetch, \"_download\", pages)` — private. So it's acceptable in this codebase.\n\nHmm, but maybe better to name it without underscore so it's a legitimate public surface? The task says \"各自抛不同异常类型\" — a public classification function is defensible: `raise_for_pubmed_status(status: int, *, context: str) -> None`. Hmm, adding public API just for tests is over-engineering. I'll keep it private and test it